## 5. Node/backend runtime

Install **Windows Node.js 22.22.2** (including its bundled npm). This version was confirmed on the production system. Run `npm ci` where a lockfile is present and `npm install` otherwise, separately in each of:

```text
backend\socket_server
backend\product_server
backend\report_server
backend\printer_server
backend\barcode_server
```

Each directory's `package.json` is authoritative. Socket.IO is deliberately aligned at exact Node `socket.io`/`socket.io-client` **4.8.3** and confirmed-production frontend `python-socketio` **5.16.1**. These version numbers differ because they are separate implementations, but they share Socket.IO protocol 5 and Engine.IO protocol 4. Keep all checked-in lockfiles.

```powershell
npm install pm2 -g
npm install pm2-windows-startup -g
pm2-startup install
```

`backend/ecosystem.config.js` points to nonexistent `./app.js`, so start the actual entries, each from its directory:

```powershell
pm2 start socketServer.js --name hosangadi-socket
pm2 start productServer.js --name hosangadi-product
pm2 start reportServer.js --name hosangadi-report
pm2 start printerServer.js --name hosangadi-printer
pm2 start barcodeServer.js --name hosangadi-barcode
pm2 save
```

PM2 must run as the prepared Windows user. The commands above are each executed after changing to the matching service directory.

